<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yonago</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yonago is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori-prefecture">Tottori Prefecture</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> coast near the border with Shimane, in the former province of Hoki. Its castle, completed in the early 17th century, was held under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori">Tottori</a> domain by the Arao family, senior retainers of the Ikeda lords, who governed Hoki on their behalf. It grew into the main commercial town of western Tottori.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yonago.jpg" alt="Yonago" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1,2,6 by 663highland, 3,4,5 by Lemon · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yonago_montage_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yonago lies in the far west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori-prefecture">Tottori Prefecture</a> and faces the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> to the north and Lake Nakaumi to the northwest. It adjoins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimane-prefecture">Shimane Prefecture</a> and stands across the lake from the Shimane capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsue">Matsue</a>. The city limits are mostly flat, with the Hino River flowing through the Yonago Plain, while the southern part is hilly country at the foot of Mount Daisen. An irrigation canal called the Yonekawa carries water from the Hino River to Sakaiminato. The name is written with two kanji, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and child.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area belonged to the ancient province of Hōki. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kojiki">Kojiki</a>, the tomb of the creator kami Izanami lies on the border of Yonago with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izumo-province">Izumo Province</a>, and many remains of the Yayoi and Kofun periods have been found within the city limits.</p><p>In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> appointed Nakamura Kazutada daimyō of the 175,000 koku Yonago Domain, and the castle was reconstructed. The centre of the modern city grew from the castle town. After Nakamura died without an heir the domain was abolished and its lands passed to the Ikeda clan of Tottori Domain, who kept the castle and gave it to their hereditary senior retainers of the Arao clan, who ruled it until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p><p>The town of Yonago was established in Aioi District in October 1889, under the new system of municipalities. A post office opened in 1872, a prison in 1877 and a courthouse in 1884, and railway services began in 1902. Yonago became a city on 1 April 1927 and absorbed the neighbouring town of Yodoe on 31 March 2005.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Yonago is the second largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori-prefecture">Tottori Prefecture</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori">Tottori</a> and the commercial centre of its western part. Most of its workforce is employed in services, and Tottori University has a campus there. Its National Historic Sites include the ruins of Yonago Castle, the Mukoeyama Kofun Cluster, the Mukibanda Yayoi remains and the Kamiyodo temple ruins. Kaike Onsen, on Miho Bay, is said to be the birthplace of the triathlon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The city is twinned with Baoding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and Sokcho in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yonago” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The domain governed Hoki through a senior retainer family stationed at <b>Yonago</b> castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/tottori-domain">Tottori Domain</a></li></ul></section>
